Subject: FreeBSD: read vm.overcommit as the bit set it is
Fixes: B4 - unix_detect_overcommit took any non-zero vm.overcommit as "overcommit allowed", the inverse of its meaning
Upstream: to report

FreeBSD's vm.overcommit is a bit set (sys/vm/vm.h at freebsd-src
e23c13a0c967, lines 154-156): bit 0, SWAP_RESERVE_FORCE_ON, enforces swap
reservation (strict accounting), and bit 1, SWAP_RESERVE_RLIMIT_ON, enforces
RLIMIT_SWAP. The default, 0, allows overcommit. mimalloc v3.6.1 set
os_overcommit = (val != 0): it took the default host as strict and a strict
host as overcommitting. On a strict host (the appliance image may be one)
the default arena_eager_commit=2 then committed whole arenas up front and
charged their full size against swap. The patch tests the two bits the way
FreeBSD's own jemalloc does (contrib/jemalloc/src/pages.c, lines 616-617,
same revision). It changes one assignment, on FreeBSD only. STXlib also
sets MI_DEFAULT_ARENA_EAGER_COMMIT=0 on FreeBSD (CMakeLists.txt), so the
patch corrects the detection itself; the other users of has_overcommit now
see the host as it is: the page map's up-front commit (src/heap/page-map.c
97), page commit on demand (src/arena/arena-page.c 815), commit statistics
(src/arena/arena-alloc.c 108) and MAP_NORESERVE where it is defined
(src/prim/unix/prim.c 435).

--- a/src/prim/unix/prim.c
+++ b/src/prim/unix/prim.c
@@ -161,7 +161,10 @@
     int val = 0;
     size_t olen = sizeof(val);
     if (sysctlbyname("vm.overcommit", &val, &olen, NULL, 0) == 0) {
-      os_overcommit = (val != 0);
+      // vm.overcommit is a bit set (FreeBSD sys/vm/vm.h): bit 0 is SWAP_RESERVE_FORCE_ON
+      // (strict swap accounting) and bit 1 SWAP_RESERVE_RLIMIT_ON (RLIMIT_SWAP enforced).
+      // Memory is overcommitted only when neither is set; 0 is the default.
+      os_overcommit = ((val & 3) == 0);
     }
   #else
     // default: overcommit is true
